WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.467
An API is a contract, not merely a JSON document.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:10.259
Read the weather request as a combination of method,
target, headers and body, and interpret the

00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:13.160
response using status and representation.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.698
Document units and errors as carefully as success.

00:00:16.698 --> 00:00:23.419
HTTP conventions let clients and infrastructure
cooperate when the API follows their semantics.

00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:26.970
Use a lost-response scenario to understand
idempotence.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.103
The operation may already have happened when the
client retries.

00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:35.946
Stable resource identity makes repeated PUT or
DELETE requests predictable.

00:00:35.946 --> 00:00:42.403
Compare deleting the latest post with deleting a
specific post, and remember that different response

00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:46.277
codes can still accompany the same idempotent server
effect.
